6. NERDC CURRICULUM VERSION AND LEARNING-OBJECTIVE RESOLUTION

Implement authoritative curriculum resolution for every recorded taught topic.

The platform must not assume that there is one timeless NERDC curriculum.

Support the applicable curriculum version based on:

- Education stage.
- Class level.
- Subject.
- Academic session.
- Effective date or implementation cohort.
- Applicable curriculum release/version.

A taught topic must resolve to an actual topic record in the applicable curriculum version.

A taught topic must also resolve to the relevant learning objectives or learning outcomes associated with that topic.

Use existing NERDC curriculum data and identifiers wherever available.

Do not create invented curriculum identifiers or silently map a teacher's free-text topic to an unrelated official topic.

If the teacher enters a topic that cannot be reliably resolved:

1. Mark the topic as unresolved.
2. Explain the problem to the teacher.
3. Provide an authorized correction or mapping workflow.
4. Prevent unresolved topics from becoming verified assessment evidence until resolved.

Store the exact curriculum version and topic/objective references used at the time of teaching.

Where the curriculum changes later, previously recorded teaching evidence must retain its original curriculum reference.

Do not silently remap historical teaching records to a newer curriculum version.

The user interface should display the resolved curriculum topic and objectives so the teacher can verify that the correct material has been recorded.

