9. TEACHER-ONLY ASSESSMENT-SCOPE AUTHORIZATION

The assigned subject teacher must have complete control over the academic scope of the AI-generated assessment.

Implement a persistent assessment-scope authorization workflow.

Before generation, the assigned teacher must be able to:

1. Select the assessment type.
2. View the verified topics actually taught.
3. View the learning objectives associated with those topics.
4. Select which eligible topics to assess.
5. Select which eligible learning objectives to assess.
6. Choose the number and types of questions.
7. Choose the objective/theory balance.
8. Set the total marks.
9. Select the difficulty distribution.
10. Set the examination duration and relevant instructions.
11. Review the final assessment scope.
12. Explicitly authorize the scope before AI generation.

Assessment authorization must be recorded persistently.

It must include:

- Authorized teacher identity.
- Tenant and school instance.
- Class and section.
- Subject.
- Academic session and term.
- Curriculum version.
- Selected verified topic IDs.
- Selected learning-objective IDs.
- Source teaching-delivery evidence references.
- Assessment type.
- Marks and question configuration.
- Difficulty mix.
- Authorization timestamp.
- Authorization status.
- Scope version.
- Audit information.

Only the assigned teacher may authorize the academic scope.

A principal, administrator, AI model or system process must not silently broaden the authorized scope.

Where an administrator has a legitimate override capability under existing school policy, it must be an explicit, separately permissioned and fully audited workflow. It must not be represented as the teacher's authorization.

If the teacher changes the authorized scope after generation, create a new authorization version and preserve the previous version for audit purposes.

